#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151932 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ad76e6b-acef-5221-8852-71dc42625bf3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Correct Function Used to Define a Double Integral Over a Non-Rectangular Domain"}] :question/id #uuid "5fe8dafe-b03c-54b6-a737-6572f59b97f9" :question/math-academy-id "e-6995" :question/problem "Consider the region $D$ shown above. If $R=[-1,1] \\times [0,1]$, and\n$\\iint\\limits_D \\mid x-y \\mid \\: \\text{d}A = \\iint\\limits_R f(x,y) \\: \\text{d}A$,\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2616/Source/Images/8e46da282ec5e52c3797796b84fba298.png)\n\n1. ${\\begin{vmatrix}x - y &,\\end{vmatrix}(x, y) ∈ R∖D; 0, \\mid (x, y) ∈ D∖R$\n2. ${0, \\mid (x, y) ∈ R∖D;\\begin{vmatrix}x - y &,\\end{vmatrix}(x, y) ∈ D$\n3. ${\\begin{vmatrix}x - y &,\\end{vmatrix}(x, y) ∈ R∩D; 0, \\mid (x, y) ∉ D$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R$:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2616/Source/Images/f75faf2d7defb332a3de4e495f71609f.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely.\n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $\\mid x-y \\mid$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_D \\mid x-y \\mid \\: \\text{d}A = \\iint\\limits_R f(x,y) \\: \\text{d}A\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- We cannot use function I. This functions are not identically zero for all points in $R\\,\\backslash D$. For example, $(x,y) = (1,0) \\in R\\,\\backslash\\, D$, but the function is nonzero at this point.\n- Functions II and III equal $\\mid x-y \\mid$ on $D$ and equal $0$ outside $D$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151933 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "624215ff-efdc-5b12-8d61-9d66371672d9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Valid Region to Define a Double Integral Over a Non-Rectangular Domain"}] :question/id #uuid "80f011ae-c472-5dfa-8303-70396ecf51fd" :question/math-academy-id "e-6996" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14050897/assets/7325a35720157e2331ade1b9abd042f90030361c1560dd3bf98de08cc495e4c4.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14050897/assets/d1457393a0b919a208da95e0b819b50a83847d2a5dfbb9c4c81c4c457a2cd467.png)\nRequire $D\\subseteq R$, so the rectangle contains the entire region. Which of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\n- $R=[-2,2] \\times [-2,1]$\n\n- $R=[-2,1] \\times [-2,2]$\n\n- $R=[-1,1] \\times [-1,1]$" :question/worked-solution "For this question, $R$ must contain every point of $D$. Extending the integrand by zero on $R\\setminus D$ then preserves the integral: the function equals the original integrand throughout $D$ and contributes zero outside it. The rectangles must therefore be checked for containment, as explicitly required in the prompt.\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14050897/assets/8e0d0d508d61352edf461f147cc561a527f6b3191e6c6b11d070eedf1e6572e3.png)\n\nthen, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_D (x^2-y^2) \\: \\text{d}A = \\iint\\limits_R f(x,y) \\: \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangles I and II cover the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Rectangle III does not cover the region $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151934 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "100ab73e-3dad-5b15-9da2-d244c67bcc9b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Double Integrals Over Non-Rectangular Domains"}] :question/id #uuid "fe8eb478-6830-55bc-84c6-668924daaefc" :question/math-academy-id "e-6997" :question/problem "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curve $x^2+y^2=9$. If\n${\\cos (x - y),\\begin{vmatrix}(x, y) ∈ R∩D \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}(x, y) ∈ R∖D$,\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{\\large R} \\cos(x-y) \\: \\text{d}A = \\iint\\limits_{\\large D} f(x,y) \\: \\text{d}A,\\quad$ where $R=[-4,4] \\times [-4,4]$\n2. $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{\\large D} \\cos(x-y) \\: \\text{d}A = \\iint\\limits_{\\large R} f(x,y) \\: \\text{d}A,\\quad$ where $R=[-2,2] \\times [-2,2]$\n3. $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{\\large D} \\cos(x-y) \\: \\text{d}A = \\iint\\limits_{\\large R} f(x,y) \\: \\text{d}A,\\quad$ where $R=[-3,3] \\times [-3,3]$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D$:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2616/Source/Images/d90282a665fd8f2c55197c22b38a931a.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define\n\n$$\n{\\cos (x - y),\\begin{vmatrix}(x, y) ∈ R∩D \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}(x, y) ∈ R∖D\n$$\n\nthen, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{\\large D} \\cos(x-y) \\: \\text{d}A = \\iint\\limits_{\\large R} f(x,y) \\: \\text{d}A\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n- Statement II is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D$.\n- Statement III is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152747 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ad76e6b-acef-5221-8852-71dc42625bf3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Correct Function Used to Define a Double Integral Over a Non-Rectangular Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "824efefa-195b-5945-8270-db325c41b246" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151393} {:answer/id #uuid "b267ce69-f727-520e-8436-d2b61d797b6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151394} {:answer/id #uuid "ca4c22e1-3865-55ff-a6f1-9cc8c11a3fa0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151395} {:answer/id #uuid "0e71df1f-8709-51e6-b428-2b8b41cee33e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151396} {:answer/id #uuid "76517be8-8555-59b1-9be8-c26191bffc02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151397}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca4c22e1-3865-55ff-a6f1-9cc8c11a3fa0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151395} :answer-field/id #uuid "df630eba-04fb-566c-9a1a-e313478215e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151392}] :question/id #uuid "5ca2c9d4-83c1-5596-b062-1537df902124" :question/math-academy-id "q-80425" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2616/Source/Images/q-80425.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If $R = [- 2, 2] \\cdot [- 2, 2]$, and\n$∬_(D)(\\sin y + x^{2})dA = ∬_(R)f(x, y)dA$,\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x, y)$?\n\n1. ${\\sin y + x^{2},\\begin{vmatrix}(x, y) ∈ R∖D \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}(x, y) ∈ D∖R$\n2. ${0,\\begin{vmatrix}(x, y) ∈ R∖D \\\\ \\sin y + x^{2},\\end{vmatrix}(x, y) ∈ D$\n3. ${\\sin y + x^{2},\\begin{vmatrix}(x, y) ∈ R \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}(x, y) ∉ R$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152748 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "624215ff-efdc-5b12-8d61-9d66371672d9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Valid Region to Define a Double Integral Over a Non-Rectangular Domain"}] :quest